            <ArticleTitle>Vertebral Hemangiomas and Their Correlation With Other Pathologies.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E481-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/BRS.0000000000001464</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">This cross-sectional study was carried out on 196 adults (98 men and 98 women), aged between 18 and 91 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine whether vertebral hemangiomas (VHs) are associated with other spinal pathologies, metabolic diseases, cardiovascular diseases, cancer and past trauma, to shed light on their possible pathophysiology.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">VHs are the most common form of benign tumors in the spine. Their association with spinal and systemic pathologies has not yet been systematically studied.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Clinical data were gathered from full spine CT scans and medical records.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">VHs were significantly associated with disc lesions (P = 0.004), past trauma (P = 0.037), diabetes (χ = 5.179, P = 0.023), cardio-vascular diseases (χ = 8.625, P = 0.003), and cancer (χ = 5.820, P = 0.016), but not with obesity. Only medium-large size VHs were significantly associated with osteoporosis (χ = 6.695, P = 0.010).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The pattern of diseases related to VHs suggests a common cause for VH, namely, a disruption of vascular flow in the microvessels (accompanied by endothelium damage) within the vertebral body, eventually resulting in hypervascularization.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="LEVEL OF EVIDENCE" NlmCategory="METHODS">4.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
